The current state is represented by CS1 and CS0, while the trigger inputs T1 and T0 determine the next state. The next-state equations generate NS1 and NS0, which are connected to the D inputs of the flip-flops. Both flip-flops share a clock signal and update their stored values on the rising edge. The Q outputs provide the current-state values and feed back into the combinational logic.
The circuit also uses AND gates to generate the three outputs, Out2, Out1, and Out0, based on the current state. The Verilog implementation includes an active-high reset that forces the next-state inputs to 00, allowing the flip-flops to return to state 00 on the next rising clock edge.

Parts
2 D Flip-Flops: Each stores one bit of the current state and updates on the rising edge of the shared 1-bit clock signal.
3 XOR Gates: Two XOR gates calculate NS1, and one XOR gate calculates NS0.
4 AND Gates: One AND gate generates the carry term for NS1, while three AND gates generate the output signals.
2 NOT Gates: Generate the inverted state signals used by the output logic.

Inputs
T1: A 1-bit trigger input connected to the NS1 logic.
T0: A 1-bit trigger input connected to the NS0 logic and the carry AND gate.
CLK: A 1-bit clock signal connected to both D flip-flops.

Outputs
Out2: A 1-bit output generated when CS1 = 0 and CS0 = 1.
Out1: A 1-bit output generated when CS1 = 1 and CS0 = 0.
Out0: A 1-bit output generated when CS1 = 1 and CS0 = 1.

Interfaces
CS1 and CS0: Two 1-bit feedback signals from the flip-flop Q outputs that represent the current state and connect to the next-state and output logic.
NS1 and NS0: Two 1-bit signals connecting the combinational next-state logic to the D inputs of the flip-flops.
Carry: A 1-bit internal signal generated by CS0 AND T0 and connected to the second XOR gate used to calculate NS1.
